```java
// Unbuffered: a system call for every single byte -> very slow
try (var in = new FileReader("big.txt")) {
    int c;
    while ((c = in.read()) != -1) {   // hits the disk on each byte
        process((char) c);
    }
}
```

```java
// Buffered: fills an 8 KB buffer once, then serves reads from memory
try (var in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("big.txt"))) {
    String line;
    while ((line = in.readLine()) != null) { // chunked reads + line API
        process(line);
    }
}
```

*Wrapping the raw reader in a `BufferedReader` can be an order of magnitude faster for many small reads.*

Run this on a real file and watch the speedup print — the only change between the two loops is the `BufferedInputStream` wrapper.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.file.*;

public class BufferedIODemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        var configFile = Path.of("application.properties");

        // SLOW: unbuffered - every byte = one system call
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        try (var fis = new FileInputStream(configFile.toFile())) {
            while (fis.read() != -1) { } // reads byte by byte
        }
        long unbufferedTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        // FAST: buffered - reads in 8KB chunks
        start = System.nanoTime();
        try (var bis = new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream(configFile.toFile()))) {
            while (bis.read() != -1) { } // still looks byte-by-byte but the buffer handles it
        }
        long bufferedTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.printf("Unbuffered: %d ms%n", unbufferedTime / 1_000_000);
        System.out.printf("Buffered:   %d ms%n", bufferedTime / 1_000_000);
        System.out.printf("Speedup:    %.1fx faster%n", (double) unbufferedTime / bufferedTime);
    }
}
```

The buffered loop calls `read()` the same number of times, yet it touches the disk far less — the buffer absorbs the calls in memory.

The text path adds an `InputStreamReader` with an explicit charset; the binary path reads bytes straight through.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;

public class BufferedReaderVsInputStream {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Text -> BufferedReader (charset-aware, readLine)
        try (var r = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(
                new FileInputStream("notes.txt"), StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = r.readLine()) != null) System.out.println(line);
        }

        // Binary -> BufferedInputStream (raw bytes, no decoding)
        try (var in = new BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream("logo.png"))) {
            byte[] buf = new byte[8192];
            int n;
            while ((n = in.read(buf)) != -1) { /* handle n bytes */ }
        }
    }
}
```

Notice the text chain pins `StandardCharsets.UTF_8` — that is what makes it read the same on every machine.

The first log line stays in the buffer until `flush()` is called; the last one rides out on the automatic `close()`.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.time.*;

public class FlushDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        var logFile = new File("app.log");

        try (var writer = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(logFile))) {
            writer.write("[" + Instant.now() + "] Application started\n");
            // Data is in the buffer, not yet on disk!

            writer.write("[" + Instant.now() + "] Processing request\n");
            writer.flush(); // Force write to disk NOW
            // Data is now on disk (assuming no OS buffer issues)

            writer.write("[" + Instant.now() + "] Request completed\n");
            // close() automatically calls flush(), so this line is written too
        } // try-with-resources calls close() which calls flush()

        System.out.println("Check app.log - all entries written");
    }
}
```

If the JVM died between the first write and the `flush()`, that first line would never reach disk.
